The co-change flags are not a selection rule. A seeded random sample of
30 never-audited files (median 2 body commits since the docstring, none
flagged) drifted at >= 19/30 on 2026-10-06, against 57-89% for flagged
files across runs. What drifts there cannot be seen from blame: a fold
or move in another file, or a docstring describing wiring that never
landed. So each audit now also reads the N files whose last recorded
review is oldest.

- `--rolling N` lists them (never-reviewed first, oldest docstring
  first; then by review date), beyond the flagged set, skipping
  generated code. It reports ledger coverage and rows whose file is
  gone. It never changes the exit code.
- `--record-review FILE ... --run LABEL [--date]` records reviews,
  matches included (a read that finds nothing leaves no commit). Every
  path is validated before anything is written; exit 2 otherwise.
- `.ci/docstring-review-ledger.json` is seeded with 189 files: those
  edited by the nine audit fix commits since 2026-09-25, the files the
  lab notebook records as read-and-matched, and the 2026-10-06 sample.
  185 scanned files have no recorded review.

The ledger records reviews rather than inferring them from blame,
because blame cannot tell an audit fix from a mass stamp (floor_facts).

